      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Case report."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To present a previously undescribed complication of bone cement use during spinal surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Surgical cement reaching the circulation is a frequently described complication. The placement of caval vein filters is a routine measure in specific patients to prevent thrombotic material, usually from deep venous thrombosis in the lower extremities, from reaching the pulmonary circulation. A case of bone cement getting trapped in the caval filter rendering it unremovable has not been published."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A 66-year-old patient underwent dorsal spondylodesis of the lumbar spine for superinfected metastasis with instability. Because of deep venous thrombosis of both femoral and iliac veins, a caval filter had been placed before surgery. Bone cement from the surgical procedure migrated into the venous bloodstream and got caught in the caval filter, thus rendering the filter unremovable."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Surgical removal of the filter was necessary."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "If caval filters are present in patients undergoing surgery involving the use of bone cement, the possibility of cement being caught by the filter must be considered."
        }
      ]},
